111251.254: [11:12:51]# Edit this file to introduce tasks to be run by cron.
111251.254: [11:12:51]# 
111251.254: [11:12:51]# Each task to run has to be defined through a single line
111251.254: [11:12:51]# indicating with different fields when the task will be run
111251.254: [11:12:51]# and what command to run for the task
111251.254: [11:12:51]# 
111251.254: [11:12:51]# To define the time you can provide concrete values for
111251.254: [11:12:51]# minute (m), hour (h), day of month (dom), month (mon),
111251.254: [11:12:51]# and day of week (dow) or use '*' in these fields (for 'any').
111251.254: [11:12:51]# 
111251.254: [11:12:51]# Notice that tasks will be started based on the cron's system
111251.254: [11:12:51]# daemon's notion of time and timezones.
111251.254: [11:12:51]# 
111251.255: [11:12:51]# Output of the crontab jobs (including errors) is sent through
111251.255: [11:12:51]# email to the user the crontab file belongs to (unless redirected).
111251.255: [11:12:51]# 
111251.255: [11:12:51]# For example, you can run a backup of all your user accounts
111251.255: [11:12:51]# at 5 a.m every week with:
111251.255: [11:12:51]# 0 5 * * 1 tar -zcf /var/backups/home.tgz /home/
111251.255: [11:12:51]# 
111251.255: [11:12:51]# For more information see the manual pages of crontab(5) and cron(8)
111251.255: [11:12:51]# 
111251.255: [11:12:51]# m h  dom mon dow   command
